==English==

===Etymology===
Uncertain in origin. These have been suggested:
* From Irish {{term|sionnachuighim|lang=ga||I play the fox}},
* From Spanish {{term|chanada||lang=es}}, a shortening of {{term|charranada||lang=es|trick", "deceit}},
* From German slang {{term|schenigeln||lang=de|to work}} from peddler’s argot {{term|Schenigelei|lang=de||work}}.

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# [[trickery|Trickery]], [[games]]; [[skulduggery]]. To "call", "claim" or "declare" shenanigans is to rhetorically and ironically label something as officially deceitful, improper, or otherwise incorrect.
#: ''"The advertisement said it would cost $50 but they charged me $75 at the register. I declare '''shenanigans'''."'' The speaker passes off the statement as having some sort of implied official effect, but in reality, it is simply a complaint.
# General silliness, especially if taken to an unusual degree, not necessarily of devious intent.
#: So, Zach went to class today in a cat outfit, and he's totally aware it's not Halloween. He's always up to shenanigans.
# A deceitful confidence trick, or mischief causing discomfort or annoyance. 
# Mischievous play, especially by children.
#: ''You should learn to spot their '''shenanigans''' and avoid being fooled.''

====Usage notes====
* Much more common in its plural form.

